<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Commonwealth of Nations</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Commonwealth of Nations is a voluntary association of independent states, most of them former territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. It evolved from the self-governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> of the empire in the first half of the twentieth century. It provided a framework for continued cooperation after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a> and is also mentioned in connection with former colonies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a> that later passed through British administration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations.jpg" alt="Commonwealth of Nations" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CMallwitz at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marlborough_House.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…first self-governing dominion of the British Empire, and its gradual, negotiated path from colony to independence, through responsible government, Confederation and the Statute of Westminster, became a model for Australia, New Zealand and South Africa and for the later transformation of the empire into the <b>Commonwealth of Nations</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Rwanda joined the <b>Commonwealth of Nations</b> in 2009 as one of its few members with no historical link to the British Empire, and in 2008 it changed the medium of instruction in its schools from French to English.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rwanda">Rwanda · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Decolonisation turned most of the empire into independent states, many of which joined the <b>Commonwealth of Nations</b> and kept parliamentary institutions on the Westminster model, English common law and the English language.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>English, French, Spanish and Portuguese became languages of government across much of the world, and the <b>Commonwealth of Nations</b>, the Francophonie and the Community of Portuguese Language Countries keep old ties alive.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>For 55 years Barbados was a <b>Commonwealth</b> realm with the British monarch as head of state.</blockquote><a href="/state/barbados">Barbados</a></li><li><blockquote><b>British Commonwealth</b> forces drove the Axis from Cyrenaica in November 1942 and took Tripoli in January 1943.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-military-administration-of-libya">British Military Administration of Libya</a></li></ul></section>
